1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,408
Frame generation can double your FPS counter.

2
00:00:03,688 --> 00:00:05,726
So why does the game feel the same?

3
00:00:06,476 --> 00:00:12,286
The GPU renders two real frames, then builds a predicted frame that goes between them.

4
00:00:12,566 --> 00:00:14,574
Your screen gets twice the pictures.

5
00:00:15,324 --> 00:00:20,030
But only real frames come from the game, and that's where your mouse gets read.

6
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:24,811
The inserted frame is a guess, so it can't react to anything you just did.

7
00:00:25,561 --> 00:00:30,575
60 real frames shown as 120 still respond like 60.

8
00:00:30,855 --> 00:00:36,644
Building that middle frame also means holding back the newest real one, adding a little delay.

9
00:00:36,924 --> 00:00:39,473
That's why NVIDIA pairs it with Reflex.

10
00:00:40,223 --> 00:00:46,643
AMD recommends at least 60 real frames per second first, and says avoid it below 30.

11
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:49,029
It's smoothness, not speed.

12
00:00:49,309 --> 00:00:53,652
The real fixes are in our video, What Actually Raises FPS.

